\title{Shoreline Retreat Rates from Multi-Decadal Aerial Photo
Analysis\thanks{Draft for internal circulation.}}
\author{S.~Aoyama, R.~Delgado, and P.~Nilsen}
\date{April 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We derive shoreline retreat rates for a 14~km fetch of mixed
sand--gravel coast from nine epochs of aerial photography
spanning 1963--2024.
Digitised proxy shorelines are cast into transect-wise retreat
rates with a weighted regression that accounts for positional
uncertainty in the older photography.
Retreat is strongly localised: three eroding reaches account for
most of the sediment loss, while intervening reaches are
near equilibrium.
The results support a reach-scale sediment budget for the
planned nourishment scheme.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/intro}

\input{sections/methods}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}
Retreat rates vary markedly along the fetch
(Table~\ref{tab:rates}).
The three northernmost reaches (R1--R3) retreated at sustained
rates between 0.6 and 1.1~m/yr over the full record, whereas
the central reaches (R4--R6) show rates statistically
indistinguishable from zero once positional uncertainty is
propagated.
The southern reach R7 shows a modest advance in the most recent
eepoch pair, attributable to the 2011 groyne rehabilitation.
Pooling the eroding reaches, the total sediment loss over
1963--2024 is estimated at 1.9~Mm$^3$, with the largest
decadal losses in the 1970s and the 2010s.
The weighted regression residuals show no sign of acceleration
at the fetch scale, although reach R2 shows a locally
significant increase after 2005 that coincides with the
abandonment of the small harbour arm at its northern end.
Uncertainty intervals widen for epoch pairs involving the
1963 photography, reflecting the weaker ground control of
that survey.
Sensitivity to the choice of proxy is modest: repeating the
analysis with the high-water line instead of the vegetation
line changes reach-averaged rates by less than 8\% and leaves
the spatial pattern intact.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Reach & Length (km) & Retreat rate (m/yr) \\
\midrule
R1 & 1.8 & $0.9 \pm 0.2$ \\
R2 & 2.1 & $1.1 \pm 0.2$ \\
R3 & 1.5 & $0.6 \pm 0.3$ \\
R4 & 2.4 & $0.1 \pm 0.2$ \\
R5 & 2.2 & $0.0 \pm 0.2$ \\
R6 & 2.0 & $-0.1 \pm 0.2$ \\
R7 & 2.0 & $-0.2 \pm 0.3$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Reach-averaged retreat rates, 1963--2024}
\label{tab:rates}
\end{table}

\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}
Multi-decadal aerial photography supports reach-scale retreat
estimates with uncertainties small enough to inform a sediment
budget.
Retreat on this coast is concentrated in three reaches, and the
planned nourishment should be dimensioned for those reaches
rather than spread uniformly along the fetch.
The photographic archive remains the cheapest monitoring tool
for this setting and should be extended with the announced
2027 survey.

